[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa,ispb
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: Upstream routing policy on the ISP edge routers (ispa and ispb) – specifically their default routes.
- Mechanism: PC1 reaches its default gateway (10.1.0.1 on dswa1/2) and the WAN edge (r1) and can even ping the ISP handoff (203.0.113.1). However, for any non-local “internet” destinations, r1 NATs and forwards to the ISP. Both ISP routers send those packets back toward the customer because their default routes point to r1. This creates a routing loop for all unknown internet prefixes, so web requests time out.
- Localization: The loop is beyond the customer core. From PC1, traceroute to 203.0.113.1 succeeds via dswa1 -> core -> r1 -> ispa. On ispa, “ip route” shows “default via 203.0.113.2 dev eth0” (r1). On ispb, “ip route” shows “default via 203.0.113.6 dev eth0” (r1). Pinging 8.8.8.8 from ispa yields ICMP redirect from 203.0.113.2; from ispb it returns TTL exceeded from 192.0.2.1, confirming looping behavior between ISP edges and the customer.
- Root cause: The default routes on both ISP routers were misconfigured this morning to point back at the customer edge (r1), causing a return-path loop for all external destinations.
- Corrective fix: On ispa and ispb, remove the default routes that point to r1 and set them to the proper upstream/peer path (e.g., via the inter-ISP link 192.0.2.0/30 as intended, or to the correct upstream provider). Example: ispa: ip route del default; ip route add default via 192.0.2.2; ispb: ip route del default; ip route add default via 192.0.2.1. After this, NATed traffic from 10.1.0.0/24 will exit normally and internet access from PC1 will be restored. 
=== END ===